# Summary

=== layout: bookend

# What Would Change Your Mind?

@body

- A kidney organoid has cells that resemble parts of a kidney. What would it have to do before a patient could use it?
- A donor agreed to a diabetes study. Who gets to decide whether someone can use that blood to study ancestry?
- Pick one case from tonight. What would you allow, and what result or condition would change your answer?

@citation

[The Belmont Report (1979)](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html)  |  [UNESCO, Universal Declaration on Bioethics and Human Rights](https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/universal-declaration-bioethics-and-human-rights)

